      "assistant_text": "## Answer\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions are tightly related\u2014they both express the same architectural principle: **centralize composition decisions at startup so they can be controlled, audited, and changed in one place.**\n\nHere\u0027s the connection:\n\n**[[credential-scoping]]** ensures that the Anthropic API key is loaded from the secrets file and injected directly into the child process\u0027s environment at spawn time\u2014never written into the Hub\u0027s own environment. This confines the credential to the minimal scope that needs it, preventing a compromised Hub from leaking it.\n\n**[[runtime-paths]]** applies the exact same discipline to file-system locations: every location the Hub uses (wiki root, database, secrets file, agent instruction directories) is resolved in a single path resolver at startup, validated once, and then passed downstream. No other part of the codebase is allowed to rediscover paths independently or read the working directory directly.\n\nBoth decisions reject scattering composition logic across the codebase. For credentials, this tightens your security perimeter; for paths, it makes your runtime configuration explicit, testable, and reproducible. They\u0027re two instances of the same architectural decision.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]], so you can reference it later.",
